<div class="lead">
  <p>Over 75% of resumes never reach human eyes. They're rejected by ATS (Applicant Tracking Systems) - software that scans and scores resumes before anyone reads them.</p>
  <p>This guide shows you exactly how to optimize your resume to pass ATS screening and land more interviews in 2026.</p>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>What is an ATS?</h2>
  <p>An Applicant Tracking System (ATS) is software companies use to automatically screen resumes. Think of it as a gatekeeper that decides if your resume gets seen by a human recruiter.</p>
  <p>Popular ATS platforms include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Workday</li>
    <li>Taleo (Oracle)</li>
    <li>Greenhouse</li>
    <li>Lever</li>
    <li>iCIMS</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each system works slightly differently, but they all scan for the same basic elements.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How ATS Resume Scanners Work</h2>
  <p>When you submit your resume, the ATS:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Parses your resume:</strong> Extracts text from your PDF or Word doc</li>
    <li><strong>Identifies sections:</strong> Finds your contact info, work history, education, skills</li>
    <li><strong>Matches keywords:</strong> Compares your resume against the job description</li>
    <li><strong>Scores you:</strong> Assigns a compatibility score (usually 0-100%)</li>
    <li><strong>Ranks candidates:</strong> Recruiters see top-scoring resumes first</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If your score is too low, your resume goes to the reject pile - even if you're perfectly qualified.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Why Resumes Get Rejected by ATS</h2>

  <h3>1. Missing Keywords</h3>
  <p>ATS scans for specific skills, tools, and phrases from the job description. If "Python" appears 5 times in the job posting but 0 times in your resume, you score poorly.</p>

  <h3>2. Formatting Issues</h3>
  <p>Fancy formatting confuses ATS:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tables and text boxes (ATS can't read them)</li>
    <li>Headers/footers (content gets lost)</li>
    <li>Images and graphics (not scannable)</li>
    <li>Multiple columns (scrambles text order)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Wrong File Format</h3>
  <p>Submitting as PNG, JPG, or unusual file types. Most ATS prefer .docx or .pdf - check the job posting.</p>

  <h3>4. Creative Section Titles</h3>
  <p>Using "My Journey" instead of "Work Experience" confuses the parser. Stick to standard headings.</p>

  <h3>5. Spelling and Abbreviations</h3>
  <p>If the job description says "Search Engine Optimization" and you write "SEO", you might not get credit. Use both.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How to Optimize Your Resume for ATS</h2>

  <h3>1. Use Standard Section Headings</h3>
  <p>Stick to these ATS-friendly headings:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Contact Information</li>
    <li>Summary or Professional Summary</li>
    <li>Work Experience or Professional Experience</li>
    <li>Education</li>
    <li>Skills or Technical Skills</li>
    <li>Certifications (if applicable)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Mirror Job Description Keywords</h3>
  <p>Copy exact phrases from the job posting:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Job posting: "project management" → Your resume: "project management" ✓</li>
    <li>Job posting: "Python programming" → Your resume: "coding in Python" ✗</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the exact wording when you have the skill. Don't lie, but do match language.</p>

  <h3>3. Include Both Acronyms and Full Terms</h3>
  <p>Examples:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Search Engine Optimization (SEO)"</li>
    <li>"Customer Relationship Management (CRM)"</li>
    <li>"Certified Public Accountant (CPA)"</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This ensures you're found whether the ATS searches for the acronym or full term.</p>

  <h3>4. Use a Simple, Clean Format</h3>
  <p>ATS-friendly formatting:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Single column layout</li>
    <li>Standard fonts (Arial, Calibri, Times New Roman)</li>
    <li>Bullet points for duties and achievements</li>
    <li>Clear section breaks with whitespace</li>
    <li>No headers/footers for important content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Save in the Right Format</h3>
  <p>Unless specified otherwise, use .docx or .pdf. Most modern ATS handle both, but .docx is slightly safer for older systems.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Skills Section Strategy</h2>
  <p>The "Skills" section is ATS gold. This is where you pack keywords:</p>
  <p><strong>Example Skills Section:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Technical Skills:</strong> Python, Java, SQL, JavaScript, React, Node.js</li>
    <li><strong>Tools:</strong> Git, Docker, Kubernetes, Jenkins, AWS, Azure</li>
    <li><strong>Methodologies:</strong> Agile, Scrum, Test-Driven Development (TDD)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Don't just list skills - weave them into your work experience bullets too.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Work Experience Optimization</h2>
  <p>Format each job like this:</p>
  <pre>
Job Title | Company Name | Dates
- Achievement with measurable result and relevant keywords
- Another achievement with keywords from job description
- Technical skills demonstrated: [keyword 1], [keyword 2], [keyword 3]
  </pre>
  <p><strong>Good example:</strong></p>
  <pre>
Senior Marketing Manager | Tech Corp | Jan 2022 - Present
- Increased organic traffic 150% through SEO optimization and content marketing strategy
- Managed $500K digital advertising budget across Google Ads and Facebook Ads
- Led team of 5 using Agile methodology to deliver 20+ campaigns per quarter
  </pre>
</section>

