<section>
  <h2>Common ATS Optimization Mistakes</h2>

  <h3>Mistake 1: Keyword Stuffing</h3>
  <p>Don't just list every keyword in white text or random places. Recruiters see your final resume too - it needs to read naturally.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 2: Generic Resume for Every Job</h3>
  <p>Tailor your resume for each application. Change your summary and adjust keywords to match each specific job description.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 3: Forgetting Soft Skills</h3>
  <p>ATS also scans for: leadership, communication, problem-solving, teamwork. Include these naturally in your achievement bullets.</p>

  <h3>Mistake 4: Outdated Information</h3>
  <p>If the job requires "5 years experience" and your resume shows 4.5 years, explicitly state "5 years" if accurate after rounding.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Beyond ATS: Resume Tips for Humans</h2>
  <p>Once you pass ATS, a human reads your resume. Optimize for both:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quantify achievements:</strong> "Increased sales 40%" beats "Increased sales"</li>
    <li><strong>Action verbs:</strong> Start bullets with Led, Managed, Developed, Implemented</li>
    <li><strong>Keep it concise:</strong> 1 page for &lt;10 years experience, 2 pages max for senior roles</li>
    <li><strong>Proofread:</strong> Typos hurt you with both ATS and humans</li>
  </ul>
</section>

